Below is a comprehensive interview-oriented guide.

1. Spring Bean Scopes

Spring provides 6 bean scopes in the standard Spring framework.

    | Scope.      | Number | Meaning                                    |
    | ------------|--------| -------------------------------------------|
    | singleton   | 1      | One bean instance per Spring IoC container |
    | prototype   | Many   | New instance every time requested          |
    | request     | Many   | One instance per HTTP request              |
    | session     | Many   | One instance per HTTP session              |
    | application | Many   | One instance per ServletContext            |
    | websocket   | Many   | One instance per WebSocket session         |

The first two are available in normal Spring applications. The last four are web-aware scopes.

1.1 Singleton

Default Spring scope.

java
@Component
@Scope("singleton")
public class PaymentService {
}

You normally don't need @Scope because singleton is the default.

java
@Component
public class PaymentService {
}

Spring creates one instance:

ApplicationCon       |
       +---- PaymentService instance
                  ^
                  |
          Service A
          Service B
          Controller

Example:

java
ApplicationContext context =
        new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);

PaymentService p1 = context.getBean(PaymentService.class);
PaymentService p2 = context.getBean(PaymentService.class);

System.out.println(p1 == p2);

Output:

true

Important interview point

Singleton means:

> One instance per Spring IoC container, not necessarily one instance per JVM.

2. Prototype Scope

java
@Component
@Scope(ConfigurableBeanFactory.SCOPE_PROTOTYPE)
public class ReportGenerator {
}

Every getBean() gives a new object.

java
ReportGenerator r1 =
        context.getBean(ReportGenerator.class);

ReportGenerator r2 =
        context.getBean(ReportGenerator.class);

System.out.println(r1 == r2);

Output:

false

Important lifecycle difference

For singleton:

Spring creates
      ↓
Dependency injection
      ↓
@PostConstruct
      ↓
Bean ready
      ↓
Application shutdown
      ↓
@PreDestroy

For prototype:

Spring creates
      ↓
Dependency injection
      ↓
@PostConstruct
      ↓
Bean returned

Spring doesn't normally manage destruction of prototype beans.

