25. Multiple Beans Problem

Suppose:

java
@Component
public class StripePaymentService
        implements PaymentService {
}

java
@Component
public class PaypalPaymentService
        implements PaymentService {
}

Then:

java
@Autowired
private PaymentService paymentService;

Spring doesn't know which implementation to use.

You'll get a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException.

Use:

java
@Primary

or:

java
@Qualifier("stripePaymentService")

Example:

java
public OrderService(
        @Qualifier("stripePaymentService")
        PaymentService paymentService) {
}

26. @Primary vs @Qualifier

@Primary

Default choice.

java
@Primary
@Component
public class StripePaymentService
        implements PaymentService {
}

@Qualifier

Explicit choice.

java
public OrderService(
        @Qualifier("paypalPaymentService")
        PaymentService paymentService) {
}

27. @Lazy

Normally singleton beans are eagerly initialized.

java
@Component
@Lazy
public class ExpensiveService {
}

Now Spring creates it when first needed.

Useful for:

- Expensive initialization
- Optional functionality
- Reducing startup time

But don't use @Lazy blindly to hide circular dependencies.

