54. peek()

peek() is commonly misunderstood.

java
numbers.stream()
       .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
       .peek(n -> System.out.println("After filter: " + n))
       .map(n -> n  10)
       .peek(n -> System.out.println("After map: " + n))
       .toList();

peek() is mainly intended for:

Debugging
Observing pipeline elements

It is lazy.

This does nothing:

java
numbers.stream()
       .peek(System.out::println);

Because there is no terminal operation.

Need:

java
.toList();

or:

java
.forEach(System.out::println);

55. Should You Modify Objects Inside peek()?

Avoid this:

java
employees.stream()
         .peek(e -> e.setSalary(e.getSalary()  1.1))
         .toList();

Use:

java
.map(e -> {
    e.setSalary(e.getSalary()  1.1);
    return e;
})

Even better, prefer immutable transformation where practical.

