        employees.stream()
                 .map(Employee::getName)
                 .collect(Collectors.toList());

Modern Java can use:

java
.toList();

62. groupingBy()

Very common interview question.

Suppose:

java
class Employee {
    String department;
    String name;
}

Group employees:

java
Map<String, List<Employee>> result =
        employees.stream()
                 .collect(
                     Collectors.groupingBy(
                         Employee::getDepartment
                     )
                 );

Result:

IT → [...]
HR → [...]
Finance → [...]

63. Grouping + Counting

java
Map<String, Long> count =
        employees.stream()
                 .collect(
                     Collectors.groupingBy(
                         Employee::getDepartment,
                         Collectors.counting()
                     )
                 );

Result:

IT = 10
HR = 5
Finance = 7

64. partitioningBy()

Partitions into exactly two groups.

java
Map<Boolean, List<Integer>> result =
        numbers.stream()
               .collect(
                   Collectors.partitioningBy(
                       n -> n % 2 == 0
                   )
               );

Result:

true  → even numbers
false → odd numbers

Difference

groupingBy
    ↓
multiple groups

partitioningBy
    ↓
two groups
true / false

65. joining()

java
String result =
        List.of("Java", "Spring", "AWS")
            .stream()
            .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));

Output:

Java, Spring, AWS

66. reduce()

Used to combine multiple values into one.

Sum:

java
int sum =
        numbers.stream()
               .reduce(0, Integer::sum);

Equivalent concept:

1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6

Result:

21

Maximum:

java
Optional<Integer> max =
        numbers.stream()
               .reduce(Integer::max);

67. mapToInt()

Primitive specialization.

Instead of:

java
numbers.stream()
       .map(n -> n  2)
       .reduce(0, Integer::sum);

You can use:

java
int sum =
        numbers.stream()
               .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)
               .sum();

Other methods:

java
average()
sum()
min()
max()
count()

68. anyMatch()

java
boolean exists =
        numbers.stream()
               .anyMatch(n -> n > 5);

Returns true if at least one matches.

69. allMatch()

java
boolean result =
        numbers.stream()
               .allMatch(n -> n > 0);

All must match.

70. noneMatch()

java
boolean result =
        numbers.stream()
               .noneMatch(n -> n < 0);

No elements match.

71. findFirst()

java
Optional<Integer> result =
        numbers.stream()
               .filter(n -> n > 3)
               .findFirst();

72. findAny()

java
Optional<Integer> result =
        numbers.parallelStream()
               .filter(n -> n > 3)
               .findAny();

Especially useful when working with parallel processing where you don't require a specific first element.

73. Parallel Stream

java
numbers.parallelStream()
       .map(this::process)
       .toList();

Conceptually:

Input
 |
 +---- Thread 1
 |
 +---- Thread 2
 |
 +---- Thread 3
 |
 +---- Thread 4
 |
Combine

But parallel stream does NOT automatically mean faster.

74. When Parallel Stream Can Help

Good candidate:

Large dataset
CPU-intensive processing
Independent operations
No shared mutable state
Enough CPU cores

Example:

java
long count =
        hugeList.parallelStream()
                .filter(this::expensiveCalculation)
                .count();

75. When NOT to Use Parallel Stream

Avoid blindly using it for:

Small collections
I/O operations
Database calls
HTTP calls
Shared mutable state
Ordering-sensitive logic
Latency-sensitive workloads without benchmarking

This is especially important in backend enterprise applications.

For example, don't do:

java
users.parallelStream()
     .map(user -> restClient.callExternalService(user))
     .toList();

as a generic concurrency strategy.

You can overwhelm:

External service
Connection pool
CPU
Common ForkJoinPool

76. parallelStream() Uses Which Pool?

By default, parallel streams use the common ForkJoinPool.

This is an important interview question.

java
list.parallelStream()

doesn't automatically create a dedicated thread pool for your operation.

Therefore, be careful in server-side applications.

77. Stream Laziness

Intermediate operations are lazy.

java
numbers.stream()
       .filter(n -> {
           System.out.println(n);
           return n > 3;
       })
       .map(n -> n  2);

Nothing happens.

Add:

java
.toList();

Now execution occurs.

78. Intermediate vs Terminal Operations

Intermediate

Return another Stream:

filter
map
flatMap
distinct
sorted
peek
limit
skip

Terminal

End the pipeline:

collect
toList
forEach
reduce
count
min
max
findFirst
findAny
anyMatch
allMatch
noneMatch

79. Stream Short-Circuiting

These can stop processing early:

findFirst
findAny
anyMatch
allMatch
noneMatch
limit

Example:

java
boolean result =
        numbers.stream()
               .filter(n -> n > 10)
               .anyMatch(n -> n % 2 == 0);

Once a matching even number is found, processing can stop.

