81. One Complete Stream Chain

This is the kind of chain you should be comfortable explaining in an interview:

java
Map<String, List<String>> result =
        employees.stream()

                // 1. Remove inactive employees
                .filter(Employee::isActive)

                // 2. Observe data for debugging
                .peek(e ->
                    System.out.println(
                        "Active: " + e.getName()))

                // 3. Extract department
                .map(Employee::getDepartment)

                // 4. Remove duplicate departments
                .distinct()

                // 5. Sort
                .sorted()

                // 6. Limit result
                .limit(10)

                // 7. Convert each department
                .map(String::toUpperCase)

                // 8. Collect
                .collect(
                    Collectors.groupingBy(
                        department -> department,
                        Collectors.toList()
                    )
                );

But note: this example artificially combines many operations. Don't chain methods just to make a long chain. In production, optimize for readability.

82. map() vs flatMap() — Must Know

| map() | flatMap() |
|---|---|
| One-to-one transformation | One-to-many transformation |
| Doesn't flatten | Flattens nested streams |
| Stream<A> → Stream<B> | Stream<A> → Stream<B> after flattening |
| Simple transformation | Nested collection processing |

Example:

java
List<String> names =
        employees.stream()
                 .map(Employee::getName)
                 .toList();

versus:

java
List<String> skills =
        employees.stream()
                 .flatMap(e -> e.getSkills().stream())
                 .toList();

83. map() vs peek()

Very common interview question.

map

Used for transformation.

java
.map(String::toUpperCase)

peek

Used mainly for observation/debugging.

java
.peek(System.out::println)

Don't use peek() as your primary business transformation mechanism.

84. forEach() vs peek()

forEach

Terminal operation.

java
stream.forEach(System.out::println);

peek

Intermediate operation.

java
stream.peek(System.out::println)
      .toList();

85. Stream vs Collection

Collection:

Stores data

Stream:

Processes data

Collection:

java
List<Integer> numbers =
        List.of(1, 2, 3);

Stream:

java
numbers.stream()
       .filter(...)
       .map(...)
       .toList();

A Stream does not generally store the elements itself.

86. Stream Can Usually Be Consumed Only Once

This fails:

java
Stream<Integer> stream =
        numbers.stream();

stream.count();

stream.forEach(System.out::println);

You'll get:

IllegalStateException:
stream has already been operated upon or closed

Create another stream:

java
numbers.stream().count();
numbers.stream().forEach(...);

87. Stream Interview Rapid-Fire

Memorize these:

What is Stream?

A pipeline abstraction for declarative processing of data.

Is Stream a data structure?

No.

Does Stream modify the original collection?

Normally no, unless your lambda explicitly mutates contained objects/external state.

Are intermediate operations lazy?

Yes.

Is filter() intermediate?

Yes.

Is map() intermediate?

Yes.

Is flatMap() intermediate?

Yes.

Is peek() intermediate?

Yes.

Is collect() terminal?

Yes.

Is forEach() terminal?

Yes.

Is reduce() terminal?

Yes.

Is sorted() stateful?

Yes.

Is distinct() stateful?

Yes.

Is limit() short-circuiting?

Yes.

Is findFirst() short-circuiting?

Yes.

Does parallelStream() guarantee faster execution?

No.

What pool does parallel stream commonly use?

Common ForkJoinPool.

